B.aDq=new A.bo("Have you ever been to London?","Hi\xe7 Londra'ya gittin mi?",null,null)
B.aEl=new A.bo("I haven't finished the book yet.","Kitab\u0131 hen\xfcz bitirmedim.",null,null)
B.bat=s([B.atd,B.aAH,B.aDq,B.aEl],t.M6)
B.aLb=new A.c0("I have lived in Ankara in 2018.","I have lived in Ankara since 2018. / I lived in Ankara in 2018.","Present perfect belirli ge\xe7mi\u015f zaman ifadesiyle (in 2018) gelmez. Belirli zaman varsa past simple.",'Present perfect cannot be used with specific past time expressions like "in 2018". If the time is pinpointed, use past simple.')
B.aNN=new A.c0("I have went there.","I have gone there. / I have been there.",'"have/has" sonras\u0131 V3 (past participle) gelir, V2 de\u011fil. go \u2192 went (V2) \u2192 gone (V3).',"After have/has, you need the past participle (V3), not the simple past (V2). The forms are: go \u2192 went (V2) \u2192 gone (V3).")
B.aN5=new A.c0("Did you ever see this movie?","Have you ever seen this movie?",'"ever" (hayat\u0131n boyunca) deneyim sorusudur \u2014 present perfect ister.','"Ever" signals a life-experience question, which requires the present perfect, not the past simple.')
B.bl7=s([B.aLb,B.aNN,B.aN5],t.gr)
B.cdo=new A.f8("Present Perfect \u2014 Yak\u0131n Ge\xe7mi\u015f / Tecr\xfcbe","Ge\xe7mi\u015fte ba\u015flam\u0131\u015f, etkisi \u015fu ana uzanan veya zaman\u0131 belirsiz olaylar. T\xfcrk\xe7ede kar\u015f\u0131l\u0131\u011f\u0131 tam yok \u2014 bu y\xfczden T\xfcrkler en \xe7ok burada hata yapar.","have/has + V3 (past participle)\nOlumsuz: haven't/hasn't + V3   \xb7   Soru: Have/Has + \xf6zne + V3?","\u2022 \u015eimdiye uzanan deneyim: I have visited Italy three times. (hayat\u0131mda)\n\u2022 Az \xf6nce biten: She has just finished.\n\u2022 Devam eden durum: We have lived here for 5 years. (h\xe2l\xe2 ya\u015f\u0131yoruz)\n\u2022 Belirsiz zaman: Have you ever eaten sushi?",B.bat,B.bl7,'\u015eu sihirli soruyu sor: "Belirli bir ge\xe7mi\u015f zaman var m\u0131?" Varsa (yesterday, last week, in 2019) \u2192 past simple. Yoksa (ever, never, just, yet, since, for) \u2192 present perfect.',"Present Perfect","Used for past events whose effects extend to the present, or for experiences at an unspecified time. Turkish has no direct equivalent, making this tense one of the most error-prone for Turkish learners.","\u2022 Life experience: I have visited Italy three times.\n\u2022 Recently completed action: She has just finished.\n\u2022 Ongoing situation: We have lived here for 5 years. (still living there)\n\u2022 Unspecified time: Have you ever eaten sushi?",'Ask yourself: "Is there a specific past time reference?" If yes (yesterday, last week, in 2019) \u2192 use past simple. If no (ever, never, just, yet, since, for) \u2192 use present perfect. Turkish speakers often default to past simple for both, so this check is essential.',"have/has + V3 (past participle)\nNegative: haven't/hasn't + V3   \xb7   Question: Have/Has + subject + V3?")
