B.aP4=new A.c0("You should to study.","You should study.",'Modaller do\u011frudan V1 al\u0131r, "to" gerekmez. (\u0130stisna: ought to, have to.)','Modal verbs are followed directly by the base verb \u2014 no "to" in between. (Exceptions: ought to and have to.)')
B.aMc=new A.c0("I must to go.","I must go.",'"must" sonras\u0131 "to" YOK. Ama "have to go" do\u011fru.','"Must" takes no "to". However, "have to go" is correct because "have to" is a semi-modal, not a pure modal.')
B.aRe=new A.c0("She cans speak French.","She can speak French.","Modaller 3. tekilde -s almaz.","Modal verbs never add -s in the third person singular.")
B.byc=s([B.aP4,B.aMc,B.aRe],t.gr)
B.cdr=new A.f8("Modal Yard\u0131mc\u0131lar: can/should/must/might","Yetenek, zorunluluk, izin, olas\u0131l\u0131k ve tavsiye i\xe7in kullan\u0131lan yard\u0131mc\u0131 fiiller. Hepsinin T\xfcrk\xe7e kar\u015f\u0131l\u0131klar\u0131 farkl\u0131.","modal + V1 (yal\u0131n fiil)\ncan/could (yetenek/izin) \xb7 should/ought to (tavsiye) \xb7 must/have to (zorunluluk)\nmay/might (olas\u0131l\u0131k) \xb7 will/would (gelecek/ko\u015fullu)","\u2022 can: yetenek, izin (I can swim. Can I sit here?)\n\u2022 should: tavsiye (You should sleep more.)\n\u2022 must: g\xfc\xe7l\xfc zorunluluk (You must wear a seatbelt.)\n\u2022 have to: d\u0131\u015fsal zorunluluk (I have to work tomorrow.)\n\u2022 may/might: olas\u0131l\u0131k (It may rain.)\n\u2022 would: kibar istek, ko\u015fullu (Would you help me?)",B.bcB,B.byc,'"-meli, -mal\u0131" g\xf6rd\xfc\u011f\xfcnde akl\u0131na 3 se\xe7enek gelsin: should (tavsiye), must (g\xfc\xe7l\xfc) veya have to (d\u0131\u015fsal). Hangisi c\xfcmleye uyuyor d\xfc\u015f\xfcn.',"Modal Verbs: can / should / must / might","Modal verbs express ability, obligation, permission, possibility, and advice. Each modal has a distinct meaning and nuance that must be learnt individually.","\u2022 can: ability or permission (I can swim. Can I sit here?)\n\u2022 should: advice (You should sleep more.)\n\u2022 must: strong internal obligation (You must wear a seatbelt.)\n\u2022 have to: external obligation (I have to work tomorrow.)\n\u2022 may/might: possibility (It may rain.)\n\u2022 would: polite request or conditional (Would you help me?)",'Turkish speakers often use "must" where "should" is more natural, or vice versa. The key distinction: must comes from inside (your own obligation or strong conviction), have to comes from outside (rules, laws, circumstances). Should is a recommendation. Choose based on the source of the obligation.',"modal + V1 (base verb)\ncan/could (ability/permission) \xb7 should/ought to (advice) \xb7 must/have to (obligation)\nmay/might (possibility) \xb7 will/would (future/conditional)")
B.ay2=new A.bo("If you don't hurry, you'll miss the bus.","Acele etmezsen otob\xfcs\xfc ka\xe7\u0131racaks\u0131n.","Type 1","Type 1 \u2014 real possibility")
B.aDI=new A.bo("If I had more money, I would travel.","Daha \xe7ok param olsa seyahat ederdim.","Type 2","Type 2 \u2014 hypothetical")
B.aC0=new A.bo("If she had called, I would have helped.","Aram\u0131\u015f olsayd\u0131 yard\u0131m ederdim.","Type 3","Type 3 \u2014 past counterfactual")
B.bnn=s([B.ay2,B.aDI,B.aC0],t.M6)
B.aMe=new A.c0("If I will see him, I will tell him.","If I see him, I will tell him.",'Type 1\'de "if" c\xfcmleci\u011finde "will" KULLANILMAZ. Present simple kullan.','In Type 1 conditionals, the if-clause uses the present simple, never "will". Only the result clause uses will.')
B.aNl=new A.c0("If I was you, I would go.","If I were you, I would go.",'Type 2 hayali \u015fart: t\xfcm \u015fah\u0131slarda "were" kullan\u0131l\u0131r (formal). "If I was" konu\u015fma dilinde duyulur ama yaz\u0131da "were" do\u011frudur.','In formal and written Type 2 conditionals, "were" is used for all persons, including I. "If I was" is heard in informal speech but "if I were" is standard.')
B.bi0=s([B.aMe,B.aNl],t.gr)
B.cdq=new A.f8("Conditionals \u2014 \u015eart C\xfcmleleri (if)","Be\u015f ana tip var: 0 (ger\xe7ek), 1 (olas\u0131), 2 (hayali \u015fimdi), 3 (ge\xe7mi\u015f pi\u015fmanl\u0131k), mixed (karma).","Type 0: If + present, present (genel ger\xe7ek)\nType 1: If + present, will + V1 (olas\u0131 gelecek)\nType 2: If + past, would + V1 (hayali \u015fimdi)\nType 3: If + past perfect, would have + V3 (ge\xe7mi\u015f hayal)","\u2022 Type 0: If you heat water, it boils. (her zaman)\n\u2022 Type 1: If it rains, I'll stay home. (ya\u011fma ihtimali var)\n\u2022 Type 2: If I were you, I would apologize. (sen de\u011filim)\n\u2022 Type 3: If I had studied, I would have passed. (ge\xe7mi\u015fte yapmad\u0131m)",B.bnn,B.bi0,'"-se/sa" g\xf6rd\xfc\u011f\xfcnde \xf6nce "ger\xe7ek mi hayal mi?" sor. Ger\xe7ek/olas\u0131 \u2192 Type 1. \u015eu an hayal \u2192 Type 2. Ge\xe7mi\u015f pi\u015fmanl\u0131k \u2192 Type 3.',"Conditionals \u2014 if Sentences","There are four main conditional types: zero (general truth), first (real possibility), second (hypothetical present/future), and third (past regret or counterfactual).","\u2022 Type 0: If you heat water, it boils. (always true)\n\u2022 Type 1: If it rains, I'll stay home. (realistic possibility)\n\u2022 Type 2: If I were you, I would apologise. (I am not you \u2014 hypothetical)\n\u2022 Type 3: If I had studied, I would have passed. (I didn't study \u2014 past regret)","Turkish speakers sometimes mix conditional types because Turkish uses a single -se/sa suffix for all of them. Ask two questions: (1) Is it real or hypothetical? (2) Present or past? Real + present = Type 1. Hypothetical + present = Type 2. Hypothetical + past = Type 3.","Type 0: If + present, present (general truth)\nType 1: If + present, will + V1 (likely future)\nType 2: If + past, would + V1 (imaginary present)\nType 3: If + past perfect, would have + V3 (past imaginary)")
