B.aN_=new A.c0("She wants going home.","She wants to go home.",'"want" her zaman to + V1 al\u0131r.','"Want" always requires an infinitive (to + base verb). It never takes a gerund.')
B.blg=s([B.aNL,B.aN_],t.gr)
B.cdK=new A.f8("Gerund vs Infinitive: -ing veya to + V1","Baz\u0131 fiiller sonras\u0131nda -ing al\u0131r, baz\u0131lar\u0131 to + V1, baz\u0131lar\u0131 her ikisi. Liste ezberlenir.","Gerund (V+ing) alanlar: enjoy, finish, avoid, suggest, mind, practice, miss...\nInfinitive (to V1) alanlar: want, need, decide, hope, promise, plan, learn, agree...\nHer ikisi: like, love, hate, start, begin, continue (anlam fark\u0131 \xe7ok k\xfc\xe7\xfck)","\u2022 I enjoy reading. (gerund \u2014 enjoy \u2192 -ing)\n\u2022 I want to read. (infinitive \u2014 want \u2192 to)\n\u2022 I like cooking. = I like to cook. (her ikisi OK)",B.bcr,B.blg,'En s\u0131k 20 fiili gruplara ay\u0131r ve ezberle. Yeni fiil \xf6\u011frenirken c\xfcmle i\xe7inde \xf6\u011fren: "I suggested going" gibi.',"Gerund vs Infinitive","Some verbs are followed by a gerund (-ing), others by an infinitive (to + base verb), and some accept both. These patterns must be memorised in groups.","\u2022 I enjoy reading. (gerund \u2014 enjoy always takes -ing)\n\u2022 I want to read. (infinitive \u2014 want always takes to)\n\u2022 I like cooking. = I like to cook. (both forms accepted)",'Turkish speakers often struggle because Turkish uses a single verbal noun form. Group the most common verbs by pattern and memorise them as chunks: enjoy/finish/avoid/suggest + -ing; want/need/decide/hope/plan + to. Learning new verbs in full sentences (e.g. "I suggested going") makes the pattern stick.',"Take a gerund (V+ing): enjoy, finish, avoid, suggest, mind, practice, miss...\nTake an infinitive (to V1): want, need, decide, hope, promise, plan, learn, agree...\nBoth: like, love, hate, start, begin, continue (very little difference in meaning)")
