B.aKk=new A.c0("I have a car red.","I have a red car.","\u0130ngilizcede s\u0131fat isimden \xd6NCE gelir.","In English, adjectives always come before the noun they describe, unlike in Turkish where they also precede but other word order norms differ.")
B.b9p=s([B.aJs,B.aKk],t.gr)
B.cdJ=new A.f8("Kelime S\u0131ras\u0131: SVO","\u0130ngilizce s\u0131k\u0131 Subject-Verb-Object d\xfczenidir. T\xfcrk\xe7edeki esnek dizilime al\u0131\u015fk\u0131n olanlar burada hata yapar.","\xd6zne + Y\xfcklem + Nesne + (Yer) + (Zaman)\nI (S) ate (V) pizza (O) at home (P) yesterday (T).","\xc7o\u011fu c\xfcmlede zorunlu s\u0131ra. S\u0131fatlar isimden \xf6nce gelir.",B.bA7,B.b9p,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "D\xfcn ben okula gittim" / "Ben d\xfcn okula gittim" hepsi olur. \u0130ngilizcede SVO s\u0131ralamas\u0131n\u0131 koru, zaman ifadelerini en sona at.',"Word Order: SVO","English follows a strict Subject\u2013Verb\u2013Object order. Speakers of Turkish, which has flexible word order, often rearrange elements incorrectly.","Mandatory in most sentences. Adjectives always precede the noun they modify.","Turkish has free word order because case endings carry grammatical meaning. English relies entirely on position, so the Subject\u2013Verb\u2013Object sequence is non-negotiable in most sentences. Put time expressions at the end, and keep subject and verb together.","Subject + Verb + Object + (Place) + (Time)\nI (S) ate (V) pizza (O) at home (P) yesterday (T).")
B.aHi=new A.bo("She is taller than her brother.","Erkek karde\u015finden daha uzun.",null,null)
