B.aMG=new A.c0("I said him the truth.","I told him the truth.",'"tell" do\u011frudan ki\u015fi al\u0131r. "say" ile "to him" gerekir: "I said the truth to him".','"Tell" is always followed directly by a person (tell him, tell me). "Say" requires "to" before the person: I said it to him.')
B.aMz=new A.c0("I borrowed him my book.","I lent him my book.","lend = \xf6d\xfcn\xe7 vermek, borrow = \xf6d\xfcn\xe7 almak. T\xfcrk\xe7ede tek kelime ama \u0130ngilizcede iki ayr\u0131 fiil.",'"Lend" means to give temporarily; "borrow" means to receive temporarily. Turkish has a single word for both, which causes confusion for Turkish speakers.')
B.bxj=s([B.aMG,B.aMz],t.gr)
B.cdM=new A.f8("Kelime Se\xe7imi (Word Choice)","Anlam\u0131 yak\u0131n ama kullan\u0131m\u0131 farkl\u0131 kelimeler: do/make, say/tell, listen/hear, look/see/watch, lend/borrow...","do (eylem) vs make (\xfcretmek)\nsay (s\xf6ylemek) vs tell (birine s\xf6ylemek)\nlisten (aktif) vs hear (pasif)\nlook at (bakmak) vs see (g\xf6rmek) vs watch (izlemek)","\u2022 do homework, do the dishes (rutinler)\n\u2022 make a decision, make dinner (\xfcretim)\n\u2022 I said hello. / I told him a story.\n\u2022 Listen to music. / I heard a noise.",B.b43,B.bxj,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "yapmak" geni\u015f bir fiil. \u0130ngilizcede do/make fark\u0131 var. "make" yeni bir \u015fey ortaya \xe7\u0131karmakt\u0131r (dinner, decision, mistake). "do" zaten var olan eylemdir (homework, dishes, exercise).',"Word Choice","Words with similar meanings but different usage patterns: do/make, say/tell, listen/hear, look/see/watch, lend/borrow. These pairs must be learnt as collocations.","\u2022 do homework, do the dishes (routine activities)\n\u2022 make a decision, make dinner (creating or producing something)\n\u2022 I said hello. / I told him a story. (say alone vs tell + person)\n\u2022 Listen to music (active) / I heard a noise. (passive \u2014 happens to you)",'Turkish speakers often overuse "do" or "make" because Turkish covers both with a single verb (yapmak). The key distinction: "make" involves creating or producing something new (make dinner, make a decision, make a mistake); "do" refers to activities or tasks that already exist as concepts (do homework, do the dishes, do exercise). When in doubt, check a collocation dictionary.',"do (perform an action) vs make (create something)\nsay (utter words) vs tell (tell someone)\nlisten (active) vs hear (passive)\nlook at (direct your eyes) vs see (perceive) vs watch (follow over time)")
