B.aMw=new A.c0("By 2025 I will finish university.","By 2025 I will have finished university.",'"by + gelecek zaman" o ana kadar tamamlanmay\u0131 anlat\u0131r \u2192 future perfect gerekir.','"By + future time" signals completion before that point, which requires the future perfect (will have + V3), not the simple future.')
B.byg=s([B.aMw],t.gr)
B.cdH=new A.f8("Future Perfect \u2014 Gelecekte Tamamlanm\u0131\u015f Zaman",'Gelecekteki belirli bir andan \xd6NCE tamamlanm\u0131\u015f olacak eylemler. "By + zaman" ifadeleriyle s\u0131k kullan\u0131l\u0131r.',"will have + V3 (past participle)\nOlumsuz: won't have + V3   \xb7   Soru: Will + \xf6zne + have + V3?",'\u2022 Gelecekte bir ana kadar bitecek: By 2030, I will have finished my studies.\n\u2022 "by the time" ile: By the time you arrive, I will have cooked dinner.',B.bfq,B.byg,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "-m\u0131\u015f olaca\u011f\u0131m" yap\u0131s\u0131na denk gelir. "By" g\xf6rd\xfc\u011f\xfcnde "will have + V3" d\xfc\u015f\xfcn.',"Future Perfect",'Used for actions that will be completed before a specific point in the future. Commonly paired with "by + time" expressions.','\u2022 Completed before a future point: By 2030, I will have finished my studies.\n\u2022 With "by the time": By the time you arrive, I will have cooked dinner.','Whenever you see "by + time" in a future context, reach for will have + V3. Turkish speakers often default to simple future (will + V1) because the Turkish equivalent "-m\u0131\u015f olaca\u011f\u0131m" feels complex and is sometimes avoided in speech.',"will have + V3 (past participle)\nNegative: won't have + V3   \xb7   Question: Will + subject + have + V3?")
B.aCx=new A.bo("I gave up smoking last year.","Ge\xe7en y\u0131l sigaray\u0131 b\u0131rakt\u0131m.","give up = vazge\xe7mek","give up = to quit")
B.aFY=new A.bo("Can you turn down the music?","M\xfczi\u011fi k\u0131sar m\u0131s\u0131n?","turn down = k\u0131smak","turn down = to reduce volume")
B.atn=new A.bo("We ran out of milk.","S\xfct\xfcm\xfcz bitti.","run out of = t\xfckenmek","run out of = to use up the supply")
B.azv=new A.bo("She looks after her little brother.","K\xfc\xe7\xfck karde\u015fine bakar.","look after = bakmak","look after = to take care of")
B.bpT=s([B.aCx,B.aFY,B.atn,B.azv],t.M6)
B.aN6=new A.c0("Turn off it.","Turn it off.","Zamir (it/them) ayr\u0131labilir phrasal verb'de fiil ile par\xe7ac\u0131k ARASINA girer.","When the object is a pronoun (it/them), it must go between the verb and the particle in separable phrasal verbs.")
B.b3j=s([B.aN6],t.gr)
B.cdn=new A.f8("Phrasal Verbs \u2014 Deyimsel Fiiller","Fiil + edat/zarf birle\u015fince yeni bir anlam kazan\u0131r (give up = vazge\xe7mek). G\xfcnl\xfck \u0130ngilizcenin bel kemi\u011fidir; anlam\u0131 par\xe7alardan tahmin edilemez.","fiil + par\xe7ac\u0131k (up, out, on, off, in, down, away\u2026)\nAyr\u0131labilir: turn it off  \xb7  Ayr\u0131lamaz: look after him","\u2022 G\xfcnl\xfck konu\u015fma: I get up at seven. / Turn off the light.\n\u2022 Resm\xee olmayan yaz\u0131, sohbet, film/dizi.",B.bpT,B.b3j,"Tek tek ezberleme \u2014 anlam k\xfcmeleri halinde \xf6\u011fren (get up/get on/get over). Bir phrasal verb g\xf6rd\xfc\u011f\xfcnde resm\xee kar\u015f\u0131l\u0131\u011f\u0131n\u0131 da not al (find out = discover).","Phrasal Verbs","A verb combined with a particle (preposition or adverb) takes on a new meaning that cannot be predicted from the parts alone. Phrasal verbs are the backbone of everyday English.","\u2022 Everyday speech: I get up at seven. / Turn off the light.\n\u2022 Informal writing, conversation, films and TV series.","Turkish speakers sometimes try to use a single formal verb (discover instead of find out) to avoid phrasal verbs, but this sounds unnatural in everyday conversation. Learn phrasal verbs in semantic clusters: get up / get on / get over / get by. Also note whether each one is separable or inseparable \u2014 the position of pronoun objects depends on this.","verb + particle (up, out, on, off, in, down, away\u2026)\nSeparable: turn it off  \xb7  Inseparable: look after him")
B.auS=new A.bo("The book that I read was great.","Okudu\u011fum kitap harikayd\u0131.",null,null)
B.aCo=new A.bo("She's the doctor who treated me.","Beni tedavi eden doktor o.",null,null)
B.awr=new A.bo("This is the house where I grew up.","Buras\u0131 b\xfcy\xfcd\xfc\u011f\xfcm ev.",null,null)
B.bxE=s([B.auS,B.aCo,B.awr],t.M6)
B.aPO=new A.c0("The man which helped me was kind.","The man who helped me was kind.",'Ki\u015filer i\xe7in "which" de\u011fil "who/that" kullan\u0131l\u0131r.','Use "who" or "that" for people, and "which" or "that" for things. "Which" alone is never used for people.')
B.aLt=new A.c0("The girl who she sat next to me.","The girl who sat next to me.",'\u0130lgi zamiri zaten \xf6zne \u2192 ayr\u0131ca "she" koyma (\xe7ift \xf6zne hatas\u0131).','The relative pronoun "who" already serves as the subject of the clause \u2014 adding "she" creates a double subject error.')
B.bzA=s([B.aPO,B.aLt],t.gr)
B.cdk=new A.f8("Relative Clauses \u2014 \u0130lgi C\xfcmlecikleri","who/which/that/whose/where ile bir ismi a\xe7\u0131klayan yan c\xfcmle. \u0130ki c\xfcmleyi tek, ak\u0131c\u0131 bir c\xfcmlede birle\u015ftirir.","ki\u015fi \u2192 who/that  \xb7  \u015fey \u2192 which/that  \xb7  iyelik \u2192 whose  \xb7  yer \u2192 where","\u2022 \u0130smi tan\u0131mlama: The man who called you is my boss.\n\u2022 Ekstra bilgi (virg\xfcll\xfc): My car, which is red, is old.",B.bxE,B.bzA,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "-en/-an" ekiyle yap\u0131lan s\u0131fat-fiil (gelen ki\u015fi) \u0130ngilizcede relative clause olur: the person who comes. T\xfcrk\xe7ede s\u0131fat isimden \xd6NCE, \u0130ngilizcede SONRA gelir.',"Relative Clauses","A subordinate clause introduced by who, which, that, whose, or where that modifies a noun. It combines two sentences into one fluent unit.","\u2022 Defining (no commas): The man who called you is my boss.\n\u2022 Non-defining (with commas, extra info): My car, which is red, is old.",'Turkish speakers are used to placing modifying clauses before the noun (gelen adam = the man who comes). In English, relative clauses come after the noun: the man who comes. Also remember not to repeat the subject pronoun inside the clause \u2014 "who" does that job already.',"person \u2192 who/that  \xb7  thing \u2192 which/that  \xb7  possession \u2192 whose  \xb7  place \u2192 where")
B.au4=new A.bo("You live here, don't you?","Burada ya\u015f\u0131yorsun, de\u011fil mi?",null,null)
B.azR=new A.bo("She isn't coming, is she?","O gelmiyor, de\u011fil mi?",null,null)
B.aAN=new A.bo("They've finished, haven't they?","Bitirdiler, de\u011fil mi?",null,null)
B.b8Q=s([B.au4,B.azR,B.aAN],t.M6)
B.aPQ=new A.c0("You are happy, isn't it?","You are happy, aren't you?",'"isn\'t it?" her c\xfcmleye eklenmez. Tag, c\xfcmlenin yard\u0131mc\u0131 fiili + \xf6znesiyle uyumlu olmal\u0131.','"Isn\'t it?" is not a universal tag in English. The tag must match the auxiliary verb and subject of the main clause.')
B.b7x=s([B.aPQ],t.gr)
B.cdz=new A.f8("Question Tags \u2014 Onay Sorusu Ekleri","C\xfcmlenin sonuna eklenen k\u0131sa soru (\u2026de\u011fil mi?). Olumlu c\xfcmle \u2192 olumsuz tag, olumsuz c\xfcmle \u2192 olumlu tag.","Olumlu c\xfcmle, + olumsuz yard\u0131mc\u0131 + \xf6zne?\nYou are tired, aren't you?  \xb7  She can't swim, can she?","\u2022 Onay/teyit isteme: It's cold today, isn't it?\n\u2022 Sohbeti s\xfcrd\xfcrme, nazik soru.",B.b8Q,B.b7x,'T\xfcrk\xe7edeki "de\u011fil mi?" tek kal\u0131pt\u0131r ama \u0130ngilizcede tag, c\xfcmleye g\xf6re de\u011fi\u015fir. \xd6nce c\xfcmlenin yard\u0131mc\u0131 fiilini bul, sonra tersini al.',"Question Tags","A short question added to the end of a statement to seek confirmation. A positive statement takes a negative tag; a negative statement takes a positive tag.","\u2022 Seeking confirmation: It's cold today, isn't it?\n\u2022 Keeping conversation going, making polite requests.",'Turkish has a single tag ("de\u011fil mi?") for all statements, but English tags must match the auxiliary verb of the main clause and then be reversed in polarity. Step 1: identify the auxiliary (is, are, was, were, do, did, have, can\u2026). Step 2: reverse it (positive \u2192 negative, negative \u2192 positive). Step 3: use the subject pronoun.',"Positive sentence, + negative auxiliary + subject?\nYou are tired, aren't you?  \xb7  She can't swim, can she?")
B.aD1=new A.bo("I used to live in Ankara.","Eskiden Ankara'da ya\u015fard\u0131m.",null,null)
B.aAi=new A.bo("She didn't use to like coffee.","Eskiden kahveyi sevmezdi.",'olumsuzda "use to"','negative: "use to" without -d')
B.awf=new A.bo("Did you use to have long hair?","Eskiden sa\xe7\u0131n uzun muydu?",null,null)
B.b92=s([B.aD1,B.aAi,B.awf],t.M6)
B.aRf=new A.c0("I use to go there.","I used to go there.",'Olumlu c\xfcmlede "used to" (-d ile). Yaln\u0131zca olumsuz/soruda "use to".','In positive sentences, always write "used to" (with -d). Only in negatives and questions does it become "use to" (no -d), because "didn\'t/did" already carries the past marker.')
B.aJP=new A.c0("I didn't used to smoke.","I didn't use to smoke.",'"did" zaten ge\xe7mi\u015f \u2192 ard\u0131ndan yal\u0131n "use to" gelir.','"Did" already marks the past tense, so the following verb returns to its base form: "use to", not "used to".')
B.bnw=s([B.aRf,B.aJP],t.gr)
B.cds=new A.f8("Used to \u2014 Eskiden Yapma Al\u0131\u015fkanl\u0131\u011f\u0131",'Ge\xe7mi\u015fte d\xfczenli yap\u0131lan ama art\u0131k yap\u0131lmayan eylemler/durumlar. "Eskiden \u2026-erdim" anlam\u0131.',"used to + V1 (yal\u0131n fiil)\nOlumsuz: didn't use to + V1   \xb7   Soru: Did + \xf6zne + use to + V1?","\u2022 Ge\xe7mi\u015f al\u0131\u015fkanl\u0131k: I used to play football every weekend.\n\u2022 Ge\xe7mi\u015f durum: There used to be a cinema here.",B.b92,B.bnw,'"used to" (al\u0131\u015fkanl\u0131k) ile "be used to" (al\u0131\u015fk\u0131n olmak) kar\u0131\u015ft\u0131r\u0131l\u0131r. used to + V1 = eskiden yapard\u0131m  \xb7  be used to + V-ing = al\u0131\u015f\u0131\u011f\u0131m.',"Used to \u2014 Past Habits",'Describes actions or states that were regular in the past but are no longer true. Equivalent to "I used to\u2026" meaning something happened habitually before but does not happen now.',"\u2022 Past habit: I used to play football every weekend.\n\u2022 Past state that no longer exists: There used to be a cinema here.",'Turkish speakers often confuse "used to" (past habit) with "be used to" (be accustomed to). They look similar but behave differently: used to + base verb (I used to run); be used to + gerund (I am used to running). Also do not confuse either with "get used to" (to become accustomed).',"used to + V1 (base verb)\nNegative: didn't use to + V1   \xb7   Question: Did + subject + use to + V1?")
