B.aJ2=new A.c0("I going to call him.","I'm going to call him.",'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "arayaca\u011f\u0131m" tek kelime oldu\u011fu i\xe7in am/is/are d\xfc\u015f\xfcyor. Bu yap\u0131n\u0131n ta\u015f\u0131y\u0131c\u0131s\u0131 "be" fiilidir; o olmadan c\xfcmle ayakta durmaz.','Turkish expresses this with a single word, so learners drop the verb "be". In English "be" carries the whole structure \u2014 without am, is or are the sentence collapses.')
B.aOt=new A.c0("She is going to comes with us.","She is going to come with us.",'\xd6zneye g\xf6re \xe7ekilen tek \u015fey am/is/are\'dir. "going to"dan sonraki fiil her zaman yal\u0131n kal\u0131r.','Only am/is/are changes with the subject. The verb after "going to" always stays in its base form.')
B.aLr=new A.c0("I'm gonna to eat something.","I'm gonna eat something.",'"gonna" zaten "going to" demektir; arkas\u0131na ikinci bir "to" gelmez. Ayr\u0131ca gonna yaln\u0131zca konu\u015fma dilidir, yaz\u0131da kullan\u0131lmaz.','"Gonna" already contains the "to", so a second "to" cannot follow it. Remember too that "gonna" belongs to speech only \u2014 never write it.')
B.bfk=s([B.aLn,B.aJ2,B.aOt,B.aLr],t.gr)
B.cdG=new A.f8("Gelecek: going to \u2014 \xd6nceden Kurulmu\u015f Plan",'Konu\u015fmadan \xd6NCE verdi\u011fin kararlar ve g\xf6z\xfcnle g\xf6rd\xfc\u011f\xfcn kan\u0131ta dayanan tahminler i\xe7in. T\xfcrk\xe7ede "yapaca\u011f\u0131m" tek bi\xe7imdir; \u0130ngilizce bu i\u015fi going to ile will aras\u0131nda payla\u015ft\u0131r\u0131r.',"am/is/are + going to + V1\nOlumsuz: am/is/are + not + going to + V1\nSoru: Am/Is/Are + \xf6zne + going to + V1?\nGe\xe7mi\u015fte kalan niyet: was/were going to + V1","\u2022 \xd6nceden verilmi\u015f karar: I'm going to start the gym on Monday.\n\u2022 Kan\u0131ta dayal\u0131 tahmin: Look at the sky \u2014 it's going to snow.\n\u2022 Niyet: I'm going to tell her the truth tonight.\n\u2022 Ger\xe7ekle\u015fmeyen plan: I was going to call you, but I fell asleep.",B.byj,B.bfk,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "yapaca\u011f\u0131m" tek bi\xe7im, \u0130ngilizcede iki. Kendine tek soru sor: "Bu karar\u0131 a\u011fz\u0131m\u0131 a\xe7madan \xf6nce mi verdim?" Cevap evetse going to. Bir de \u015funu unutma: fiilden \xf6nce gelen going to konu\u015fmada "gonna" diye s\xf6ylenir, ama arkas\u0131ndan bir yer geliyorsa asla k\u0131salt\u0131lmaz \u2014 "I\'m going to London".',"Future with going to",'Used for decisions you made before you started speaking, and for predictions based on evidence you can actually see. Turkish has a single future form, whereas English divides the work between "going to" and "will".',"\u2022 Decision made in advance: I'm going to start the gym on Monday.\n\u2022 Prediction based on evidence: Look at the sky \u2014 it's going to snow.\n\u2022 Intention: I'm going to tell her the truth tonight.\n\u2022 A plan that did not happen: I was going to call you, but I fell asleep.",'Turkish uses one future form where English uses two. Ask yourself a single question: "Did I decide this before I opened my mouth?" If yes, use going to. One more thing worth knowing: before a verb, "going to" is pronounced "gonna" in everyday speech, but it is never reduced when a place follows it \u2014 "I\'m going to London", not "I\'m gonna London".',"am/is/are + going to + V1\nNegative: am/is/are + not + going to + V1\nQuestion: Am/Is/Are + subject + going to + V1?\nUnfulfilled past intention: was/were going to + V1")
B.aEj=new A.bo("Can I have some water, please?","Biraz su alabilir miyim?",'kafede k\u0131saca "a water" da duyars\u0131n; o "a glass of water"\u0131n k\u0131sas\u0131','in a cafe you may hear the short form "a water", meaning "a glass of water"')
B.ax7=new A.bo("How many brothers do you have?","Ka\xe7 erkek karde\u015fin var?","say\u0131labilen \u2192 many",'countable noun, so "many"')
B.aGr=new A.bo("There isn't much time left.","Fazla vakit kalmad\u0131.","time say\u0131lamaz \u2192 much",'"time" is uncountable here, so "much"')
B.aw_=new A.bo("Let me give you some advice.","Sana bir tavsiye vereyim.",'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "bir tavsiye" olur, \u0130ngilizcede "an advice" olmaz',"Turkish counts this noun; English does not \u2014 advice is always uncountable")
B.aC9=new A.bo("We need a few chairs and a little paint.","Birka\xe7 sandalyeye ve biraz boyaya ihtiyac\u0131m\u0131z var.","a few \u2192 say\u0131labilen, a little \u2192 say\u0131lamayan",'"a few" for countable, "a little" for uncountable')
B.bi8=s([B.aEj,B.ax7,B.aGr,B.aw_,B.aC9],t.M6)
B.aL4=new A.c0("He gave me a good advice.","He gave me some good advice. / He gave me a piece of advice.",'"advice" say\u0131lamaz: ne "a" al\u0131r ne \xe7o\u011ful olur. Tek bir tanesini vurgulamak istiyorsan "a piece of advice" dersin.','"Advice" is uncountable: it takes neither "a" nor a plural -s. To point at one single item, use "a piece of advice".')
B.aPC=new A.c0("I have many homeworks today.","I have a lot of homework today.","homework, information, furniture, news, money, bread \u2014 T\xfcrk\xe7ede rahat\xe7a \xe7o\u011ful yapt\u0131\u011f\u0131m\u0131z bu isimler \u0130ngilizcede k\xfctle say\u0131l\u0131r ve -s almaz.","Homework, information, furniture, news, money and bread are mass nouns in English. Turkish pluralises them freely, but in English they never take -s.")
B.aQV=new A.c0("How much people were there?","How many people were there?",'"people" say\u0131labilir bir \xe7o\u011fuldur ("person"\u0131n \xe7o\u011fulu). Sayabiliyorsan many, sayam\u0131yorsan much.','"People" is a countable plural (the plural of "person"). If you can count it, use many; if you cannot, use much.')
B.aJb=new A.c0("I don't have some money.","I don't have any money.",'Olumsuz c\xfcmlelerde ve \xe7o\u011fu soruda "some" yerine "any" gelir. "some"u teklif ve ricalarda kullan\u0131rs\u0131n: Would you like some tea?','In negatives and most questions, "any" replaces "some". Keep "some" for offers and requests: Would you like some tea?')
B.bzz=s([B.aL4,B.aPC,B.aQV,B.aJb],t.gr)
B.cdl=new A.f8("Say\u0131labilen / Say\u0131lamayan \u0130simler ve Miktar","\u0130ngilizce isimleri ikiye ay\u0131r\u0131r: tane tane sayabildiklerin (a book, two books) ve k\xfctle olarak g\xf6rd\xfckleri (water, money, advice). Bu ayr\u0131m T\xfcrk\xe7ede yok, bu y\xfczden miktar kelimeleri s\xfcrekli kar\u0131\u015f\u0131yor.","Say\u0131labilen: a/an + tekil \xb7 say\u0131 + \xe7o\u011ful \xb7 many / a few / How many?\nSay\u0131lamayan: a/an YOK, \xe7o\u011ful YOK \xb7 much / a little / How much?\n\u0130kisiyle de: some, any, a lot of, no, plenty of\nSay\u0131lamayan\u0131 saymak i\xe7in \xf6l\xe7\xfc: a piece of, a glass of, a slice of",'\u2022 Sipari\u015f verirken: Can I have some water and two sandwiches?\n\u2022 Miktar sorarken: How much sugar? / How many people?\n\u2022 Az-\xe7ok derken: a little milk (biraz s\xfct) \xb7 a few friends (birka\xe7 arkada\u015f)\n\u2022 Teklif ve ricada: Would you like some tea? (soruda bile "some")',B.bi8,B.bzz,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "iki bilgi", "bir tavsiye", "\xfc\xe7 haber" demekte hi\xe7bir sak\u0131nca yok; \u0130ngilizce bunlar\u0131n hi\xe7birini saymaz. Emin olmad\u0131\u011f\u0131n anda ka\xe7\u0131\u015f yolun haz\u0131r olsun: "some" her iki grupla da \xe7al\u0131\u015f\u0131r, "a piece of" ise say\u0131lamayan bir ismi sayman\u0131 sa\u011flar. B\xf6ylece konu\u015furken tak\u0131l\u0131p kalmazs\u0131n.',"Countable and Uncountable Nouns","English splits nouns into two groups: countable nouns you can count one by one (a book, two books) and uncountable nouns treated as a mass (water, money, advice). Turkish makes no such distinction, which is why quantity words cause so much trouble.",'\u2022 Ordering: Can I have some water and two sandwiches?\n\u2022 Asking about quantity: How much sugar? / How many people?\n\u2022 Small amounts: a little milk (uncountable) \xb7 a few friends (countable)\n\u2022 Offers and requests: Would you like some tea? ("some", even in a question)','Turkish counts nouns that English treats as mass nouns: information, advice, news, furniture, homework, bread and money take neither "a" nor a plural -s. Keep two escape routes ready for the moment you are not sure: "some" fits both groups, and "a piece of" lets you count an uncountable noun. That way you never freeze mid-sentence.',"Countable: a/an + singular \xb7 number + plural \xb7 many / a few / How many?\nUncountable: NO a/an, NO plural \xb7 much / a little / How much?\nWith both: some, any, a lot of, no, plenty of\nMeasure words for uncountables: a piece of, a glass of, a slice of")
B.aFu=new A.bo("I was watching a film when you called.","Sen arad\u0131\u011f\u0131nda film izliyordum.","uzun eylem -ing al\u0131r, kesen k\u0131sa eylem past simple","the long action takes -ing; the short interrupting action takes the past simple")
B.aHp=new A.bo("What were you doing at midnight?","Gece yar\u0131s\u0131 ne yap\u0131yordun?",'konu\u015fmada "were you" tek par\xe7a h\xe2linde akar','in speech "were you" runs together as one unit')
B.aA1=new A.bo("They weren't listening to me.","Beni dinlemiyorlard\u0131.","olumsuz: weren't + -ing","negative: weren't + -ing")
B.aHn=new A.bo("While I was waiting, I read the whole menu.","Beklerken b\xfct\xfcn men\xfcy\xfc okudum.","while, uzun olan eylemi i\u015faret eder",'"while" introduces the longer action')
B.aE9=new A.bo("It was getting dark, so we went home.","Hava karar\u0131yordu, biz de eve gittik.","\xf6nce sahne, sonra olay","background first, then the event")
B.bfe=s([B.aFu,B.aHp,B.aA1,B.aHn,B.aE9],t.M6)
B.aQO=new A.c0("When I walking in the park, I saw her.","When I was walking in the park, I saw her.",'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "y\xfcr\xfcrken" tek kelime oldu\u011fu i\xe7in "was" d\xfc\u015f\xfcyor. \u0130ngilizcede yap\u0131n\u0131n ta\u015f\u0131y\u0131c\u0131s\u0131 was/were\'dir; o olmadan c\xfcmle ayakta durmaz.','Turkish expresses "while walking" in a single word, so learners drop "was". In English was/were carries the structure \u2014 without it the sentence does not stand.')
B.aOs=new A.c0("I was knowing the answer.","I knew the answer.","know, want, like, believe, understand gibi durum fiilleri -ing almaz \u2014 ne \u015fimdiki ne de ge\xe7mi\u015f s\xfcrekli zamanda.","Stative verbs such as know, want, like, believe and understand do not take -ing, in the past continuous any more than in the present.")
B.aQa=new A.c0("We were play football all afternoon.","We were playing football all afternoon.","was/were'den sonra fiil mutlaka -ing al\u0131r; yal\u0131n fiille birle\u015fmez.","After was/were the verb must take -ing. It never combines with the base form of the verb.")
B.aJd=new A.c0("Yesterday I was going to the cinema and I was watching a film.","Yesterday I went to the cinema and watched a film.","Ba\u015ftan sona bitmi\u015f olaylar\u0131 s\u0131ral\u0131yorsan past simple kullan\u0131rs\u0131n. Past continuous olay\u0131 de\u011fil, olay\u0131n etraf\u0131ndaki sahneyi anlat\u0131r.","Use the past simple when you are listing events that started and finished. The past continuous does not tell the event itself \u2014 it paints the scene around it.")
B.b8m=s([B.aQO,B.aOs,B.aQa,B.aJd],t.gr)
B.cdh=new A.f8("Past Continuous \u2014 Ge\xe7mi\u015fte S\xfcren Eylem",'Ge\xe7mi\u015fteki bir anda devam etmekte olan eylem: "...-yordu". Hik\xe2yenin arka plan\u0131n\u0131 kurar; o arka plan\u0131 kesen k\u0131sa olay past simple ile gelir.',"was/were + V1+ing\nOlumsuz: wasn't/weren't + V1+ing   \xb7   Soru: Was/Were + \xf6zne + V1+ing?\nHik\xe2ye kal\u0131b\u0131: ... was/were + -ing  WHEN  ... past simple\nWHILE ... was/were + -ing, ... past simple","\u2022 Ge\xe7mi\u015fte bir anda s\xfcren eylem: At eight o'clock I was having dinner.\n\u2022 Kesilen eylem: I was walking home when it started to rain.\n\u2022 Ayn\u0131 anda s\xfcren iki eylem: She was cooking while I was studying.\n\u2022 Hik\xe2yeye sahne kurmak: The sun was shining and nobody was talking.",B.bfe,B.b8m,'T\xfcrk\xe7ede "-yordu" ile "-di" aras\u0131nda gidip gelmek kolayd\u0131r ama \u0130ngilizcede i\u015f b\xf6l\xfcm\xfc nettir: sahne past continuous, olay past simple. Bir de \u015funu dene: hik\xe2yene "I was ..." ile ba\u015fla. Hem dinleyiciyi i\xe7ine \xe7eker hem de as\u0131l olay\u0131 toparlaman i\xe7in sana bir saniye kazand\u0131r\u0131r.',"Past Continuous","Describes an action that was in progress at a moment in the past. It sets the background of a story, while the short event that interrupts that background is told in the past simple.","\u2022 An action in progress at a past moment: At eight o'clock I was having dinner.\n\u2022 An interrupted action: I was walking home when it started to rain.\n\u2022 Two actions running together: She was cooking while I was studying.\n\u2022 Setting the scene of a story: The sun was shining and nobody was talking.",'Turkish moves easily between its two past forms, but English keeps a clear division of labour: the past continuous paints the scene, the past simple delivers the event. Try this in conversation: open a story with "I was\u2026". It draws the listener in and buys you a second to organise the main event in your head.',"was/were + V1+ing\nNegative: wasn't/weren't + V1+ing   \xb7   Question: Was/Were + subject + V1+ing?\nStory pattern: ... was/were + -ing  WHEN  ... past simple\nWHILE ... was/were + -ing, ... past simple")
B.bGm=new A.Z(B.c9C,[B.cdG,B.cdl,B.cdh],t.mp)
