B.c9W={past_simple:0,going_to:1,comparatives:2,countable_uncountable:3,modals:4,past_continuous:5,future_simple:6,word_order:7}
B.csw=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede ge\xe7mi\u015f eki fiilin \xfcst\xfcnde durur, o y\xfczden "did" dedikten sonra fiili de ge\xe7mi\u015fe \xe7ekmek i\xe7inden gelir. Bir c\xfcmlede ge\xe7mi\u015fi yaln\u0131zca B\u0130R kez i\u015faretle: ortada did ya da didn\'t varsa fiil yal\u0131n kal\u0131r.','Turkish marks the past on the verb itself, so after you say "did" your instinct is to mark the verb as well. Mark the past only once: if the sentence has did or didn\'t, the main verb goes back to its base form.','Did you go? Not "Did you went?"',!0)
B.crY=new A.bJ("-ed'i ayr\u0131 bir hece gibi okuma: walked tek hecedir ve sonu t sesiyle biter. Fazladan hece yaln\u0131zca t ya da d sesiyle biten fiillerde \xe7\u0131kar \u2014 wanted, needed, decided.",'Do not pronounce -ed as a separate syllable. "Walked" is one syllable ending in a /t/ sound. The extra syllable appears only after verbs that already end in a t or d sound: wanted, needed, decided.','I finished early. \u2014 "finisht", one syllable.',!1)
B.csH=new A.bJ('Hik\xe2yeye ba\u015flarken zaman\u0131 bir kez \xe7\u0131pala: "Last summer...", "A couple of years ago...". Sonras\u0131nda her c\xfcmlede zaman\u0131 tekrarlamana gerek kalmaz; dinleyici seninle ge\xe7mi\u015fte kal\u0131r, sen de zinciri s\xfcrd\xfcr\xfcrs\xfcn.','Anchor the time once at the start of a story: "Last summer\u2026", "A couple of years ago\u2026". After that you do not need a time expression in every sentence \u2014 your listener stays in the past with you and you can simply keep the chain going.',"Last summer I went to Greece. We stayed a week. It rained every day.",!1)
B.b3g=s([B.csw,B.crY,B.csH],t.y6)
B.ctj=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede "yapaca\u011f\u0131m" hem plan\u0131 hem anl\u0131k karar\u0131 kar\u015f\u0131lad\u0131\u011f\u0131 i\xe7in elin hep "will"e gidiyor. Kendine tek soru sor: bu karar\u0131 a\u011fz\u0131m\u0131 a\xe7madan \xf6nce mi verdim? Cevap evetse going to.','Turkish uses one future form for both plans and spur-of-the-moment decisions, so learners reach for "will" every time. Ask yourself one question: did I decide this before I opened my mouth? If yes, use going to.',"I'm going to look for a new job. (decided last week)",!0)
B.ctd=new A.bJ('Bir fiilden \xf6nce gelen "going to" g\xfcnl\xfck konu\u015fmada "gonna" diye s\xf6ylenir \u2014 a\u011f\u0131zda, yaz\u0131da de\u011fil. Ama arkas\u0131ndan bir yer geliyorsa asla k\u0131saltma: "I\'m going to London" olur, "I\'m gonna London" olmaz.','Before a verb, "going to" becomes "gonna" in everyday speech \u2014 in the mouth, not on paper. Never reduce it when a place follows: "I\'m going to London" is fine, "I\'m gonna London" is not.',"I'm gonna call her tonight.",!1)
B.crS=new A.bJ('Ger\xe7ekle\u015fmeyen bir plan\u0131 anlatmak ya da kibarca \xf6z\xfcr dilemek i\xe7in "I was going to ..., but ..." kal\u0131b\u0131n\u0131 cebinde tut; tek hamlede hem niyetini hem gerek\xe7eni s\xf6ylersin.','Keep "I was going to\u2026, but\u2026" ready for plans that did not happen. It is the smoothest way to explain yourself: your intention and your reason arrive in a single move.',"I was going to call you, but my phone died.",!1)
B.bcP=s([B.ctj,B.ctd,B.crS],t.y6)
B.csq=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede kar\u015f\u0131la\u015ft\u0131rmay\u0131 "daha" ad\u0131nda ayr\u0131 bir kelime ta\u015f\u0131r; bu y\xfczden elin hep "more"a gidiyor ve "more cheaper" gibi \xe7ift i\u015faret \xe7\u0131k\u0131yor. Tek heceli s\u0131fatta i\u015fi ek g\xf6r\xfcr, \xfcst\xfcne kelime eklemezsin.','Turkish marks comparison with a separate word, so your hand reaches for "more" every time and you end up doubling it: "more cheaper". With a one-syllable adjective the ending does the whole job \u2014 do not add a word on top of it.','It\'s cheaper. Not "more cheaper".',!0)
B.ct3=new A.bJ('"than" c\xfcmlenin en sessiz kelimesidir: vurgu almaz, \xf6n\xfcndeki kelimeye yap\u0131\u015f\u0131r ve neredeyse kaybolur. Onu tane tane s\xf6ylemeye \xe7al\u0131\u015f\u0131rsan c\xfcmlen ezbere okunmu\u015f gibi duyulur.','"Than" is the quietest word in the sentence. It never takes stress, it glues itself to the word before it, and it nearly disappears. Pronounce it carefully and fully, and you will sound like you are reading aloud.','She\'s taller than me. \u2014 "tallerthan" runs as one unit.',!1)
B.crP=new A.bJ('Kar\u015f\u0131la\u015ft\u0131rman\u0131n \xf6n\xfcne "a bit", "a little" ya da "much" koy; hem daha do\u011fal hem daha kibar olur. "It\'s expensive" bir yarg\u0131d\u0131r, "It\'s a bit more expensive" sohbettir.','Put "a bit", "a little" or "much" in front of the comparative. It is more natural and softer at the same time: "It\'s expensive" is a verdict, "It\'s a bit more expensive" is a conversation.',"This one's a bit cheaper, and much easier to carry.",!1)
B.b5V=s([B.csq,B.ct3,B.crP],t.y6)
B.ctg=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede "bir tavsiye", "iki bilgi", "\xfc\xe7 haber" demek son derece do\u011fal; \u0130ngilizce bunlar\u0131n hi\xe7birini saymaz. advice, information, news, furniture, homework ve money ne "a" al\u0131r ne de -s.','Turkish happily counts nouns that English treats as mass nouns. Advice, information, news, furniture, homework and money never take "a" and never take a plural -s.','Can I give you some advice? Not "an advice".',!0)
B.cs7=new A.bJ('Olumlu c\xfcmlede "much" ve "many" kitap gibi durur; ger\xe7ek konu\u015fmada insanlar "a lot of" der ve bunu "a lotta" diye yap\u0131\u015ft\u0131r\u0131r. much ve many\'yi soruya ve olumsuza sakla.','In positive statements "much" and "many" sound bookish. Real speakers say "a lot of", which comes out as "a lotta". Save much and many for questions and negatives.',"There's a lot of traffic today. / Is there much traffic?",!1)
B.csN=new A.bJ('Bir ismin say\u0131l\u0131p say\u0131lmad\u0131\u011f\u0131ndan emin de\u011filsen durup d\xfc\u015f\xfcnme: "some" iki grupla da \xe7al\u0131\u015f\u0131r, "a piece of" ise say\u0131lamayan bir ismi sayman\u0131 sa\u011flar. \u0130ki ka\xe7\u0131\u015f yolun haz\u0131r olsun, ak\u0131\u015f\u0131n bozulmas\u0131n.','When you are not sure which group a noun belongs to, do not stop to think. "Some" works with both groups, and "a piece of" lets you count an uncountable noun. Two escape routes, no break in your flow.',"I need some paper \u2014 two pieces, actually.",!1)
B.bfO=s([B.ctg,B.cs7,B.csN],t.y6)
B.csp=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7edeki "-meli/-mal\u0131" hem tavsiyeyi hem zorunlulu\u011fu kar\u015f\u0131lad\u0131\u011f\u0131 i\xe7in "must" fazla kullan\u0131l\u0131yor ve kula\u011fa emir gibi geliyor. Tavsiye veriyorsan should de; must\'\u0131 kural ve ger\xe7ek mecburiyet i\xe7in sakla.','The Turkish ending -meli covers both advice and obligation, so learners overuse "must" and end up sounding like they are giving orders. If you are giving advice, say should; keep must for rules and real necessity.',"You should try this place. / Passengers must wear a seatbelt.",!0)
B.cs9=new A.bJ('Olumlu c\xfcmlenin i\xe7inde "can" vurgu almaz, ezilip ge\xe7er; vurgu ana fiildedir. As\u0131l vurgulanan "can\'t"tir; "can" yaln\u0131zca sonda ya da \u0131srar ederken tam s\xf6ylenir: "Yes, I can." Fark\u0131 duymak i\xe7in kelimenin kendisine de\u011fil, c\xfcmlenin vurgusuna kulak ver.','Inside a positive sentence "can" is unstressed and squashed almost to nothing; the stress lands on the main verb. It is "can\'t" that takes the stress, and "can" is said in full only at the end or for emphasis: "Yes, I can." To hear the difference, listen to the rhythm of the sentence, not to the word itself.',"I can SWIM. / I CAN'T swim.",!1)
B.css=new A.bJ('Tan\u0131mad\u0131\u011f\u0131n birinden bir \u015fey isterken "can" yerine "could" demeyi al\u0131\u015fkanl\u0131k h\xe2line getir; tek kelimelik de\u011fi\u015fiklikle c\xfcmlen \xe7ok daha kibar olur. Bir basamak yukar\u0131s\u0131: "Would you mind ...?"','Make "could" your default when you ask a stranger for something. Swapping one word buys a lot of politeness. One step further up: "Would you mind\u2026?"',"Could you say that again? / Would you mind repeating that?",!1)
B.bc1=s([B.csp,B.cs9,B.css],t.y6)
B.csr=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede "y\xfcr\xfcrken" tek kelimedir; \u0130ngilizcede ayn\u0131 anlam\u0131 kurarken \xf6zneyi ve was/were\'\xfc d\xfc\u015f\xfcrmek \xe7ok kolay. Kendini "When I walking..." derken yakalarsan eksik olan par\xe7a "was"t\u0131r.','Turkish packs "while walking" into a single word, so it is very easy to drop the subject and was/were in English. If you catch yourself starting with "When I walking\u2026", the missing piece is "was".',"When I was walking home, I saw her.",!0)
B.csb=new A.bJ('was ve were c\xfcmle i\xe7inde normalde vurgulanmaz; ezilip ge\xe7erler ve vurgu -ing\'li fiile d\xfc\u015fer. "What were you doing?" sorusunda "were you" tek par\xe7a h\xe2linde akar. Yaln\u0131zca c\xfcmle sonunda ya da \u0131srar ederken tam s\xf6ylenir: "Yes, I was."','Inside a sentence, was and were are normally unstressed; they get squeezed down to a weak sound and the stress lands on the -ing verb. In "What were you doing?" the words "were you" run together as one piece. Only at the end or for emphasis are they said in full: "Yes, I was."',"I was WAITING outside.",!1)
B.csx=new A.bJ('Bir an\u0131n\u0131 anlatmaya sahneyle ba\u015fla: "So I was sitting there, and...". Hem dinleyiciyi i\xe7ine \xe7eker hem de as\u0131l olay\u0131 kafanda toparlaman i\xe7in sana bir saniye kazand\u0131r\u0131r.','Open a story with a scene: "So I was sitting there, and\u2026". It pulls the listener in and, just as usefully, buys you a second to organise the main event in your head.',"So I was standing in line, and this guy just walked past everyone.",!1)
B.bws=s([B.csr,B.csb,B.csx],t.y6)
B.cso=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede "geldi\u011finde", "gelirse" kal\u0131plar\u0131 gelece\u011fi kendi i\xe7inde ta\u015f\u0131r, bu y\xfczden "When I will come" demek \xe7ok do\u011fal geliyor. \u0130ngilizcede when, if, as soon as ya da until ile kurulan zaman ve ko\u015ful c\xfcmleci\u011finde normalde will kullan\u0131lmaz \u2014 orada geni\u015f zaman gelir.','Turkish carries future meaning inside its when- and if-clauses, so "When I will come" feels perfectly natural. In a time or condition clause with when, if, as soon as or until, English does not normally use will \u2014 use the present tense there.',"When I get home, I'll call you.",!0)
B.csg=new A.bJ("Konu\u015furken \"will\" neredeyse hi\xe7 tam s\xf6ylenmez: I'll, he'll, we'll, it'll. Tam h\xe2liyle \"I will do it\" dersen bu \u0130ngilizcede \u0131srar ya da tart\u0131\u015fma gibi duyulur.","In speech \"will\" is almost never said in full: I'll, he'll, we'll, it'll. Saying \"I will do it\" in full sounds like insistence or an argument rather than a plain statement.","I'll be there in five minutes.",!1)
B.csj=new A.bJ('Tahminini "I think" ya da "probably" ile yumu\u015fat. "It will rain" kesin konu\u015fur ve yan\u0131l\u0131rsan alt\u0131nda kal\u0131rs\u0131n; "I think it\'ll rain" hem daha do\u011fal hem de sana geri ad\u0131m pay\u0131 b\u0131rak\u0131r.','Soften a prediction with "I think" or "probably". "It will rain" is a flat claim you then have to defend; "I think it\'ll rain" sounds natural and leaves you room to be wrong.',"I think it'll probably be quiet tonight.",!1)
B.b40=s([B.cso,B.csg,B.csj],t.y6)
B.csW=new A.bJ('T\xfcrk\xe7ede fiil sona gider, \u0130ngilizcede \xf6zneden hemen sonra gelir. C\xfcmleye ba\u015flarken \xf6nce \xf6zneyi ve fiili s\xf6yle, ayr\u0131nt\u0131y\u0131 arkadan ekle: "I went to the shop" \u2014 "I to the shop went" de\u011fil.','Turkish puts the verb last; English puts it right after the subject. Say the subject and the verb first, then hang the details on the end: "I went to the shop", never "I to the shop went".',"I saw her at the station yesterday.",!0)
B.csD=new A.bJ("\u0130ngilizcede yeni ve \xf6nemli bilgi c\xfcmlenin SONUNA konur, vurguyu da o al\u0131r. As\u0131l s\xf6ylemek istedi\u011fin \u015feyi sona sakla; s\u0131ralama do\u011fru olsa bile vurgu ortada kal\u0131rsa derdin kaybolur.","English puts the new, important information at the END of the sentence, and that is where the stress falls. Save the point you actually want to make for last: even with correct word order, a sentence whose stress lands in the middle loses its point.","Where did you go? \u2014 I went to ANKARA.",!1)
B.csR=new A.bJ('C\xfcmleyi T\xfcrk\xe7e s\u0131rayla kurmaya ba\u015flad\u0131\u011f\u0131n\u0131 fark edersen zorlama; dur ve ba\u015ftan al: "Sorry, I mean..." deyip \xf6zneyle yeniden ba\u015fla. Ana dili \u0130ngilizce olanlar da s\xfcrekli b\xf6yle yapar \u2014 bu hata de\u011fil, ak\u0131c\u0131l\u0131\u011f\u0131n bir par\xe7as\u0131.','If you hear yourself building a sentence in Turkish order, do not push through it. Stop and restart: say "Sorry, I mean\u2026" and begin again from the subject. Native speakers restart mid-sentence constantly \u2014 it is part of fluency, not a failure.',"I yesterday\u2026 sorry, I mean \u2014 I went there yesterday.",!1)
B.bv4=s([B.csW,B.csD,B.csR],t.y6)
B.bLd=new A.Z(B.c9W,[B.b3g,B.bcP,B.b5V,B.bfO,B.bc1,B.bws,B.b40,B.bv4],t.We)
